%erg 0.1
Title: Sitter fails on group-library items: 5926 attachments uncovered by one unhandled lazy load
Created: 2026-09-06
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-06T21:53Z claude created
2026-09-06T21:53Z claude note found while measuring ticket 0606's residue, not by a sitter sweep. The residue turned out to be this defect rather than documents native extraction cannot handle, which is what decides 0606 — so the measurement is recorded there too.

--- body ---
## Context

The sitter's own error log on the author's machine
(`~/data/Zotero/sdt-sitter-errors.jsonl`, read 2026-09-06) holds **12 673
entries, all dated 2026-09-06**, over **5 926 distinct attachments**. Of
those entries **12 611 are one exception**:

    UnloadedDataException: Item data not loaded and field 'title' not set
    for item 3/HX9BDQ5N

Library 3 is a group library. Group libraries load lazily behind an async
getter, so an item's fields are absent until awaited; the sitter reads
`title` without that await and throws before it ever asks for a pack.

**None of the 5 926 has a pack** — checked, not assumed. So this is not noisy
logging over work that succeeded anyway: it is the reason the coverage is
what it is. Against 9 108 PDF attachments in `storage/`, 5 255 carry a pack
and 3 853 do not, and this defect accounts for the bulk of that gap.

The remaining 62 entries are a different and much smaller class — "Native SDT
did not persist a current pack", 30 distinct attachments — which is a genuine
extraction failure and is **not** what this ticket is about.

**Why this matters beyond the missing packs.** The sitter's failure
accounting counts these as failures of the documents rather than as one
fixable defect of its own, so its coverage report reads as "these attachments
cannot be covered" when the truth is "the sitter never asked". A reader
deciding whether native extraction is sufficient — which is exactly what
ticket 0606 asks — gets the wrong answer from that report.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js`, `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — the
  census and sweep, where the item's fields are read.
- `~/data/Zotero/sdt-sitter-errors.jsonl` — the evidence, author's machine,
  not committed.
- Ticket 0699 (failure accounting must add up) — this is a concrete instance
  of an accounting gap, and the two should be read together.
- Ticket 0606 — its residue question is answered by fixing this.

## Actions

1. Await the item's data load before reading any field, on the path the
   census and the sweep both take. The lazy getter is the platform's
   documented behaviour for group libraries, not an error condition.
2. Re-run the sweep and confirm the class disappears from the error log
   rather than merely shrinking.
3. Separate the two error classes in the sitter's own report: an item the
   sitter failed to read is not an attachment native extraction cannot
   handle, and today both land in one bucket.

## Test

- Red first: a group-library attachment (library 3 on the author's machine
  has 5 926 of them) put through the census with the current code. Assert a
  pack is requested for it. It must fail against the unfixed tree with the
  `UnloadedDataException` above — that failure is the control, and a test
  that passes there is testing nothing.
- After the fix, the same attachment yields either a pack or a *named*
  failure of the extraction class, never a read error.
- The report counts read failures and extraction failures apart.

## Verification

- [ ] A fresh sweep's error log carries zero `UnloadedDataException`.
- [ ] The pack count over `storage/` rises, and the rise is measured rather
      than asserted: count `.zotero-sdt-cache` before and after.
- [ ] The sitter's coverage report distinguishes "not read" from "cannot
      extract".

## Invariants

- The sitter still never writes to `storage/`, never changes extraction
  preferences and never replaces a source attachment.
- Personal-library behaviour is unchanged by the fix.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The lazy load is awaited and the exception class is gone from a real
      sweep, with the red-first test having failed against the unfixed tree.
- [ ] Read failures and extraction failures are counted apart in the report.
- [ ] The residue that remains after the fix is measured, so ticket 0606 has
      the number its decision needs.
